 "verdict": "disputed",
 "derivation": "Arabic طوفان ṭūfān is Qurʾānic (7:133 and 29:14) and means primarily 'the Deluge, the Flood'. Jeffery, The Foreign Vocabulary of the Qurʾān, pp. 207-208, follows Fraenkel in deriving it from the Aramaic/Rabbinic טופנא ṭawpānā (Onkelos on Gen. 7), Syriac ܛܘܦܢܐ ṭawpānā (Peshitta Gen. 6:17, rendering κατακλυσμός) and Mandaic ṭaupana; he says the theory 'has been generally accepted' and adds, in the footnote on the same page, that the word 'can hardly be connected, however, with the Gk. τυφῶν'. Wiktionary likewise routes the Arabic word 'through Aramaic ṭōpānā (Classical Syriac ṭawpānā)'. Yule (Hobson-Jobson s.v. TYPHOON) leaves the Semitic root ṭ-w-f 'to go round' as a possible native source and suggests only that the post-classical sense 'whirlwind' — which is not in classical Arabic — may be a late loan from a modern reflex of τυφῶν. So Klein's derivation of the Arabic noun straight from Greek τυφῶν, which Nourai adopts, is a minority position; at most Greek touched the later meteorological sense, not the word.",
