nodes.append({
 "id": 14, "lang": "English", "words": "typhoon",
 "verdict": "plausible",
 "derivation": "English typhoon is a convergence of two, arguably three, words, and Nourai's arrow captures only one strand — but a real one. (a) The sense 'great cyclonic storm of the Indian Ocean and the China Sea' entered European languages through Portuguese tufão in the mid-16th century, from Arabic/Persian/Urdu ṭūfān; Yule (Hobson-Jobson s.v. TYPHOON) says 'the probability is that Vasco [da Gama] and his followers got the tufão … direct from the Arab pilots', and the first English attestations are Touffon/Tuffon (1588, Hickock's translation of Caesar Frederick). (b) Greek τυφῶν entered English separately in the 1550s as Tiphon 'whirlwind'. (c) Cantonese 大風 tai fung 'great wind' is what Klein, Skeat and Lokotsch make the source of the modern form. Etymonline's summary — Arabic/Persian tufān for the meaning, Greek typhon and Chinese tai fung for the shape — vindicates Nourai's arrow for the sense while showing it is not the whole story. Nourai's own NOTE reports Klein's Chinese derivation accurately.",
 "derivation_fa": "واژهٔ انگلیسیِ typhoon برآیندِ هم‌نشینیِ دو (بلکه سه) واژه است و پیکانِ نورایی تنها یک رشتهٔ آن را می‌گیرد — رشته‌ای واقعی اما نه تمامِ داستان. نخست، معنیِ «تندبادِ سهمگینِ اقیانوس هند و دریای چین» در میانهٔ سدهٔ شانزدهم از راهِ پرتغالیِ tufão و آن از عربی/فارسی/اردوِ «طوفان» به زبان‌های اروپایی رسید؛ یول در هابسن-جابسن می‌نویسد که به احتمالِ زیاد واسکو دا گاما و همراهانش این واژه را یکسر از ناخدایانِ عرب گرفتند و نخستین گواهانِ انگلیسی Touffon (۱۵۸۸ م) است. دوم، τυφῶν یونانی جداگانه در دههٔ ۱۵۵۰ م به‌صورتِ Tiphon «گردباد» وارد انگلیسی شد. سوم، چینیِ کانتونیِ tai fung «بادِ بزرگ» که کلاین و اسکیت و لوکوچ آن را خاستگاهِ صورتِ امروزی می‌دانند. پس ادعای نورایی برای «معنی» پذیرفتنی است اما برای «صورت» ناقص؛ یادداشتِ خودِ او دربارهٔ نظرِ کلاین درست نقل شده است.",
 "sources": [S["typhoon"]],
 "ref_check": [
   {"ref": "SKT:581", "status": "contradicts", "note": "SKT.txt leaf 557, s.v. Typhoon: \"a violent whirlwind. (Chinese) … it is a Chinese word … ta 'great', fung 'wind' … the spelling typhoon is due to confusion with the old word typhon (not uncommon in old authors), from Gk. τυφῶν, a whirlwind.\" Skeat derives the English word from Chinese and says nothing of Arabic, so he does not support the arrow as drawn. (The entry stands at printed p. ~537 of the 1911 Concise edition; Nourai's page 581 must belong to another printing.)"}
 ],
 "consulted": [
   {"src": "KLN", "where": "s.v. typhoon (leaf 885 ≈ p. 1670)", "stance": "partial", "note": "\"Chin. tai fung, lit. 'great wind'; influenced in form by Arab. tufan (whence also Port. tufão), fr. Gk. τυφῶν\" — Arabic is admitted only as an influence, exactly as the chart's NOTE reports."},
   {"src": "HJB", "where": "Hobson-Jobson s.v. TYPHOON (pp. 947-950) and Introduction", "stance": "supports", "note": "Yule ridicules the Chinese derivation ('there is no evidence that the word is in Chinese use at all') and concludes 'the probability is that Vasco and his followers got the tufão, which our sailors made into touffon and then into [typhoon]'; the Introduction lists typhoon among words 'ascribed to a Chinese origin … [that] are not Chinese at all'."},
   {"src": "LKT", "where": "Lokotsch no. 1990", "stance": "partial", "note": "\"Chin. t'ai-fung … hieraus … engl. typhoon, dtsch. Taifun, Teifun; frz. typhon. Erst über ar. ṭūfān 'heftiger Sturm' entstand pg. tufão, tofano.\" — Chinese for English, Arabic for Portuguese."},
   {"src": "WEB", "where": "Etymonline s.v. typhoon (web archive)", "stance": "supports", "note": "\"It apparently is from tufan, a word in Arabic, Persian, and Hindi meaning 'big cyclonic storm' … 'the probability is that Vasco and his followers got the tufao … direct from the Arab pilots' … Chinese (Cantonese) tai fung 'a great wind' also might have influenced the form or sense.\""}
