 "derivation": "Latin fūmus 'smoke, steam, fume' < PIE *dʰuh₂-mos, the m-formation of the root: word-initial PIE *dʰ- gives Latin f-, and the laryngeal-coloured *-uh₂- gives ū. The equation is one of the oldest and safest in Indo-European: Skt. dhūmá- 'smoke' (= Lat. fūmus exactly, and the denominative dhūmāyati = Lat. fūmāre), Lith. dūmai (pl.), Old Prussian dumis, OCS dymŭ, Gk. θυμός 'spirit, passion' (semantically 'the smoke that rises in one'). Pokorny p. 262 and Walde-Pokorny I 835 print the whole set; Watkins/AHD dheu-1 §1a: 'Suffixed extended zero-grade form *dhū-mo- … smoke, in a. Latin fumus'.",
