 "derivation": "Each of the seven is credited to this root by Watkins/AHD dheu-1: dizzy < OE dysig 'foolish' (Gmc *dus- < extended *dheus-, 'stupefied, confused'); deer < OE dēor 'animal' (Gmc *deuzam < *dheus-o- 'breathing creature'); dust < OE dūst (Gmc *duns-to- < nasalized *dhwens-); deaf < OE dēaf (Gmc *daubaz < *dhoubh-o- 'beclouded in the senses'); dumb < OE dumb (Gmc *dumbaz < nasalized *dhu-m-bho-); dove < OE *dūfe (Gmc *dūbōn, 'dark-coloured bird'); dull < OE dol / MLG dul (Gmc *dulaz < *dhwel-). The unifying idea — 'rise in a cloud, becloud, hence dull of colour, sense or wit' — is Pokorny's and Walde-Pokorny's as well, and the chart's own root gloss ('smoke, mist, dizziness, dull color') states it exactly. The one thing to keep in mind is that the group is held together by a semantic principle rather than by a single formation, and that individual members (dove, deer) have been explained otherwise by other scholars.",
